sections.body:
Irony (*l'ironie*) says the opposite of what one means, expecting the listener to understand: *Bravo, quelle réussite !* (after a failure) — *Il fait un temps magnifique !* (in pouring rain). Clues: the context, exaggeration, a mismatch between words and situation, and in speech a flat or exaggerated intonation.

Close relatives:
- *l'antiphrase* — the figure of speech behind irony: saying the opposite;
- *le second degré* — not to be taken literally: *C'était du second degré !* (I was joking / being ironic) — *prendre au premier degré* (to take literally);
- *le sarcasme* — bitter, wounding irony;
- *le cynisme* — cynical disregard for morals: *« Les promesses n'engagent que ceux qui les reçoivent. »* (Promises only bind those who receive them — a famous cynical quip.)
